(Plan 310 task 1 - `A thing called X has <entries>.` parses into the thing
 registry. Both a bare name and a quoted multi-word name are exercised, and
 the manifest entry `a function called <name>` sits alongside data fields.
 A definition declares a type: it allocates nothing and emits no code, so
 the only output here comes from the ordinary statements around it.

 The tail proves `thing` stays an ordinary identifier outside the construct
 - it is not a lexer keyword, only a contextual one.

 Declaration position (`a 'bounding box' called viewport.` and
 `Print viewport's width.`) needs the registry lookup and field access that
 land with the declarations task; those two lines live in
 tests/331_thing_declare_access.vox.

 Note for the manifest task: `circle` declares 'from radius' and nothing
 defines it. Once "a declared member nothing defines is an error" lands
 (plan 310 §4), this file needs a matching
 `To do the circle's 'from radius'` definition.

 Acceptance: two lines - defined, 42.)

A thing called point has
  a number called x is 0,
  a number called y is 0.

A thing called 'bounding box' has
  a number called width is 1,
  a number called height is 1.

A thing called circle has
  a function called 'from radius',
  a point called centre,
  a float called radius is 1.5.

Print "defined".

a number called thing is 42.
Print thing.
